nodecode-pulldown is a rewrite of anthony-maio's anthony-maio/pulldown:

  https://github.com/anthony-maio/pulldown
  commit ca16dc479714e59f8b361e8dd1c3714110b9da75
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Anthony Maio (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's one tool as a Nodecode cell. What it keeps: the
same program, the same job and the same reading of its answer. The user
installs Pulldown's Python runtime themselves and the cell runs that CLI,
"pulldown get --json-output --meta --detail ... URL", whose JSON carries the
fields the plugin's worker answered -- url, status_code, title, content,
elapsed_ms, from_cache, ok, error, and the routing record under meta.routing:
page_type, source, quality_grade and render_recommended, which the cell prints
so a page that wants a browser says so. It keeps the plugin's four arguments
with the plugin's bounds: detail minimal, readable, structured or full
(readable by default); render false unless asked; max_chars bounded in
characters, here 1000 to 40000 with 20000 the default, clipped with the cut said out
loud and how much of the page is left; a timeout_seconds from 5 through 120,
with the program killed rather than leaked when it passes; the 2 MiB page
ceiling passed to the program as --max-bytes; and the external runtime as the
user's own installation, found on PATH or named by an absolute program path,
never installed by the cell and never updated by it. The runtime's own
protections are the plugin's and are unchanged: private and loopback
addresses refused, no credentials offered to a page, no custom headers, no
proxies and no telemetry. The plugin cached pages in the plugin data
directory; the cell caches them in cache_dir or its own folder under the
Nodecode home, for the same hour.

What is left out, and why: the Hermes worker module and the plugin's
settings indirection. The plugin spawned "<python> -I -m
pulldown.hermes_worker" with a JSON request on standard input because it
needed a boundary between the harness and a runtime it did not control; a cell
may call the same program's CLI, so the JSON goes over stdout instead of
stdin and python_path becomes the path of the program itself. The plugin's
"pulldown hermes install" wrote a runtime.json beside the plugin; here the one
setting that matters, the program, is named in the cell's section. The
plugin's code mapping an error to a code (render_unavailable,
render_redirect, url_blocked, response_too_large, http_error, fetch_failed)
is not carried over as codes: the cell answers the program's own message,
with the HTTP status when there was one, which is what a person reads anyway.
Nothing of the plugin's Python is bundled; the cell runs the same Pulldown
program the plugin ran, and its own code is Common Lisp.
